WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.211
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:16.389
Unit seven: Revolutionary Movements. Today you will explain motives and aims with purpose infinitives.

4
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:19.365
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.301
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.488
Revolution.

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:30.700
Revolution means a sudden, fundamental change in political power.

8
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:34.233
The French Revolution ended absolute monarchy.

9
00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.607
Your turn. Say: Revolution.

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.526
Revolution.

11
00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:43.284
Uprising.

12
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:47.421
Uprising means an act of rebellion by many people.

13
00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:50.074
The uprising was quickly suppressed.

14
00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.870
Your turn. Say: Uprising.

15
00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:57.676
Uprising.

16
00:00:58.417 --> 00:00:59.511
Ideology.

17
00:01:00.517 --> 00:01:03.236
Ideology means a set of political beliefs.

18
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.800
Revolutionary leaders often promised a utopian ideology.

19
00:01:08.417 --> 00:01:11.243
Your turn. Say: Ideology.

20
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.252
Ideology.

21
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:17.273
Mobilization.

22
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:21.931
Mobilization means organising people to take collective action.

23
00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:25.886
Mass mobilization depended on radio broadcasts.

24
00:01:26.483 --> 00:01:29.519
Your turn. Say: Mobilization.

25
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.832
Mobilization.

26
00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:35.457
Regime.

27
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.901
Regime means a government, especially an authoritarian one.

28
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.923
The regime controlled all newspapers.

29
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:46.297
Your turn. Say: Regime.

30
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.181
Regime.

31
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:51.836
Overthrow.

32
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.266
Overthrow means to remove a ruler or government by force.

33
00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:00.846
Rebels overthrew the king in 1789.

34
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.207
Your turn. Say: Overthrow.

35
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.265
Overthrow.

36
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:09.983
Reform.

37
00:02:10.983 --> 00:02:14.109
Reform means gradual change to improve a system.

38
00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.960
Some preferred reform to revolution.

39
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.121
Your turn. Say: Reform.

40
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:23.759
Reform.

41
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:25.785
Radical change.

42
00:02:26.783 --> 00:02:30.559
Radical change means a complete and fundamental transformation.

43
00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.393
The movement demanded radical change.

44
00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:36.810
Your turn. Say: Radical change.

45
00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:41.051
Radical change.

46
00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:45.680
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:51.628
Visal, why do ordinary people risk their lives to join revolutionary movements?

48
00:02:52.717 --> 00:03:03.110
Usually they mobilize to overthrow a regime they see as unjust, and to win rights that peaceful reform has failed to deliver.

49
00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:07.122
But revolutions don't always bring justice.

50
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:20.276
True. Some leaders use revolutionary ideology to seize absolute power. The Khmer Rouge claimed to liberate the people, then destroyed millions of lives.

51
00:03:21.383 --> 00:03:23.139
So what should we learn from that?

52
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:33.668
We should study revolutions in order to understand both their hopes and their dangers, so as not to repeat the worst mistakes.

53
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:36.636
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.272
Grammar focus: Purpose infinitives.

55
00:03:40.572 --> 00:03:41.678
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:42.383 --> 00:03:45.370
People mobilize to overthrow an unjust regime.

57
00:03:46.283 --> 00:03:49.908
We study revolutions in order to understand their hopes and dangers.

58
00:03:50.817 --> 00:03:54.302
Historians compare cases so as not to repeat simple myths.

59
00:03:55.267 --> 00:04:04.130
'To' + base verb expresses purpose. 'In order to' and 'so as to' are more formal; their negatives are 'in order not to' and 'so as not to' (never 'to not' in formal writing).

60
00:04:04.883 --> 00:04:08.778
Be careful! Don't say: They protested for demand change.

61
00:04:10.733 --> 00:04:13.348
Say: They protested to demand change.

62
00:04:13.648 --> 00:04:15.729
Purpose: to + base verb.

63
00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:19.302
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.833
Why Revolutions Happen.

65
00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:27.000
Historians have long debated why some societies experience revolution while others do not.

66
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.969
Economic hardship alone is rarely sufficient; people usually mobilize to challenge a regime that has lost legitimacy.

67
00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:42.436
Revolutionary ideologies spread rapidly when they promise to replace injustice with radical equality.

68
00:04:43.433 --> 00:04:49.636
Yet movements that seize power in order to transform society completely have sometimes produced terrible violence.

69
00:04:50.633 --> 00:04:56.104
Comparing revolutions helps us to distinguish the conditions that lead to freedom from those that lead to tyranny.

70
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.958
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:04.188
They protested blank demand fair wages.

72
00:05:04.488 --> 00:05:07.700
A, for. B, to. C, for to.

73
00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:12.827
The answer is B: to.

74
00:05:13.983 --> 00:05:16.029
Choose the correct formal negative.

75
00:05:16.329 --> 00:05:22.038
A, in order to not fail. B, in order not to fail. C, in not order to fail.

76
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.401
The answer is B: in order not to fail.

77
00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.049
To remove a government by force is to blank it.

78
00:05:32.349 --> 00:05:36.350
A, reform. B, mobilize. C, overthrow.

79
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.900
The answer is C: overthrow.

80
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.816
Gradual change to improve a system is blank.

81
00:05:46.116 --> 00:05:49.676
A, reform. B, uprising. C, regime.

82
00:05:53.233 --> 00:05:55.361
The answer is A: reform.

83
00:05:56.517 --> 00:05:59.561
According to the reading, economic hardship alone is…

84
00:05:59.861 --> 00:06:06.104
A, always enough to cause revolution. B, rarely sufficient. C, the only cause.

85
00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:11.922
The answer is B: rarely sufficient.

86
00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:16.482
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.317 --> 00:06:21.754
What conditions do you think lead people to join a revolutionary movement?

88
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:40.004
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.304 --> 00:07:21.033
People usually join revolutionary movements when peaceful ways of changing an unjust system have failed. They usually mobilize to end corruption, poverty or political repression that affects their daily lives. For example, many French peasants joined the revolution in 1789 in order to escape heavy taxes and hunger. However, history shows that revolutions can be captured by extremists, as happened in Cambodia in 1975. That is why I believe gradual reform is usually safer when it is possible.

90
00:07:21.333 --> 00:07:23.031
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:23.917 --> 00:07:28.276
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose infinitives.

92
00:07:28.576 --> 00:07:34.072
Today's value is civic awareness. Know your rights, and use them to build a fairer society.

93
00:07:34.372 --> 00:07:36.851
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.151 --> 00:07:40.209
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

